  poemFont: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7c0c8e4bba7f8e14d923c4b8dba73c35, type: 2}
  poemText: "<font=natsufont>A bunch of the earlier pages are ruined! Looks and smells
    like water damage...</font>\n\n-and with the final blow to vile Vithrelaank's
    neck, the dragon fell over dead. The cohort of cowards all cried out: \"Hark!
    The dragon lies dead! Let the songs sing of Ulfnir Bone-Skin!\" Ulfnir, ever
    the arrogant man, allowed the cheers of his lessers to wash over him, polishing
    his soul. \n\n\"For I, of all men here, alone was brave enough to fight and slay
    this Wyrm, I shall have the honor of wearing its skin into battle.\" This proclamation
    from Ulfnir gave the cowardly cohort some pause: the pious among them considered
    dragon scales to be too divine for the interference of men, the industrious among
    them considered dragon scales to be too impossible a material to forge, and the
    wiser among them understood that dragon scale would provide an edge for any battle-
    a dangerous prospect for a man as boastful as this.\n\nBut not a one of the cowardly
    cohort could raise an objection, it was as Ulfnir said: he alone proved himself
    to be honorable. And so, Ulfnir began chipping away at the dragon's scales using
    the dagger of his father's father. This task lasted many moons, but eventually
    the deed was done: 450 angaids of scale stored safely in Ulfnir's laced pouch.\n\nWith
    his bounty secured, Ulfnir began searching the frosts, looking for a smith capable
    of forging the scales. His travels took him far and wide, which met him with
    at first fascination for the exotic material, that quickly turned to disappointment;
    not a soul knew what to do with the scales, and not a one would dare risk them.\n\n\"Curse
    these timid smiths to the thousandth generation! They fear a dragon that is already
    dead!\"\n\nAs he cast these shouts to Masser and Secunda above, Ulfnir noticed
    smoke billowing out of a nearby hill. Looking for the source of the fire, Ulfnir
    found a forge nestled inside the earth, gazing up to the stars above. \"Hail!
    Does this forge have a master?\" Ulfnir billowed. \"Indeed it does,\" a gravely
    voice called back, \"what would you have of me?\" The source of the gravely voice
    waltzed toward the forge, revealing a decrepit shell of a Nord.\n\n\"I come bearing
    dragon scales, seeking to bear armor worthy of my honor. I've traveled all across
    the frigid wastes of Skyrim, and not a single smith was able, or even willing.
    Are you among their number, too?\" At this, the forgemaster chuckled. \"I've
    seen thousands of 'honorable' warriors in my day. Very few were as virtuous as
    they claimed. Let me see your scales.\" Ulfnir, whose pride was slighted, revealed
    his pouch to the forgemaster- somewhat hoping that this forgemaster too would
    be incompetent; grounds for a thrashing.\n\n\"Oh! I see... These are indeed dragon
    scales. If you wish, I shall have your armor awaiting you by the time of next
    moon.\" And so, Ulfnir waited; his anger dampened by the prospect of finally
    getting that which he sought. It was as the forgemaster said: by the next moon
    a full set of dragon-scalemail awaited Ulfnir.\n\n\"What shall I give you in
    payment, forgemaster?\" The forgemaster grinned, \"Just a piece of your-\n\n<font=natsufont>Oh
    come on! It was just getting good...</font>"
